 git remote set-url origin \"$U\" && git pull -q 2>&1 | tail -2; ls; git log --oneline | …", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "cd /hutian/story-repo-wiki 2>/dev/null && U=$(cd /hutian/repo && git remote get-url origin | sed 's#/world/venue-repo.*#/world/story-repo.wiki.git#') && git remote set-url origin \"$U\" && git pull -q 2>&1 | tail -2; ls; git log --oneline | head -3; grep -n -i -E 'fig|tab|appendix|page' Outline.md | head -60"]], "output": "```console\nOutline.md\nf65c685 Outline rev 16: finite-pool wording\n5c18907 Outline rev 15: wording\n979d2d5 Outline rev 15: Fig 6 in the appendix\n1:# Outline (story-repo, revision 16 (finite-pool wording: pattern, not per-cell fit; answers idea-review story-repo#11); revision 15 (Fig 6 stays in the appendix: no float room; answers paper-repo#3); revision 14 (finite-population account of the disjoint-draw overcoverage tested; answers experiment-repo story-repo#10); revision 13 (alphafold T2 reference cell final: 4 of 4 T2 sets no gain; 55 headline rows); revision 12 (the neither-valid case of the baseline rule stated and marked; answers idea-review story-repo#9); revision 11 (E9 and the strongest-valid-baseline headline folded in; C3 by curve; forest reference n = 200); revision 10 (C8 uses the exact clipped-excess formula of Brief rev. 8); revision 9 (one account of gene_expression; strongest-valid-baseline reading rule; the clip as claim C8, E9; answers idea-review story-repo#8); revision 8 (the gene_expression gain is the package's λ clip; two PPI++ baselines; answers experiment-repo#6); revision 7 (attribution of the gene_expression gain, C3 scoring by bracketed cells, C4 clause refuted at small n; answers idea-review story-repo#7); revision 6 (first results folded in, section 8; headline arm per tier; C3 factor K/(K−1), Brief rev. 7); venue budget applied; answers idea-review #3 in story-repo)\n8:Prediction-powered inference (PPI, PPI++) turns a fixed predictor into a valid interval for E[Y]. With n labels, N unlabeled records and f fixed, the only freedom left is (a) how the labels are used to build the control variate g(Z), and (b) how the interval is built from the estimate. The classical semi-supervised / missing-data theory gives a variance floor V* = σ²_res/n + V_g/(n+N). We (1) write the exact gap between PPI++ and that floor, (2) say when learning a calibrated g from the same n labels pays for itself (break-even n*), (3) measure the headroom on the nine ppi-python sets, a synthetic suite with known truth, and (4) map where shorter intervals exist, where they do not, and where any method fails. \"PPI++ is already near the floor on most standard datasets\" is an acceptable headline; so is \"large gain on skewed or non-monotone cells\". We claim no new efficiency principle and no \"first\" (Zhang–Brown–Cai 2019; Robins–Rotnitzky–Zhao 1994; Gronsbell et al. 2022 are credited).\n14:**Arms (named; the ladder and Table 1 use exactly these).**\n22:**Compute plan (planning estimate, to be replaced by measurement).** Target total ≲ 8 CPU-h (`resources.md` estimate; one week). Allocation as a starting point: 1-D arms (A0–A7) on all nine sets ≲ 3 CPU-h (isotonic fit on n labels + prediction on capped N is ms-scale; the 380k-record census/plankton pools are capped in N, not in the pool); GBM/X arms on census_* and alphafold only, ≤ 2 n values, small trees, ≲ 3 CPU-h; synthetic suite ≲ 1.5 CPU-h; reserve for reruns. If the measured cost exceeds this, cut in this order: GBM/X arms (C6), N/n sweep on real sets, n grid points above 3000, reps in cells already decided by the margin (not below 2000 in headline cells). Analysis writes the measured budget table.\n24:**Data access and fallback.** First job of analysis: download all nine ppi-python sets in the container. If Google Drive/gdown is blocked, tell story and idea at once; the fallback is folktables / sklearn data with a **fixed predictor trained on a split disjoint from the labeled/unlabeled draws** and a stated pool; that changes what Yhat is, and C2 is then reported as a different data basis, not as the ppi-python result.\n27:Order in the paper follows weight: the real-data ladder (C2) and the break-even test (C3) come first; C1 is the setup that makes them predictable and its numerical check is short.\n31:| C2 | **Headroom on real data.** At each set's reference n, the headline arm's (A6 / B5) paired width ratio vs PPI++ and the ratio at the estimated floor, classified by the margin abov\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "641e5ad2-3d7c-54ee-8188-37dcfecb9bfc", "at": 1795691137.247624, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 78132, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "venue-repo", "actor": "venue-repo", "execution_id": "bd171ffba30c4e9272f57409c98faa4f", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "6a37fbde-fd14-539e-8365-b17772c38896", "at": 1795691137.256958, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 78133, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "venue-repo", "actor": "venue-repo", "execution_id": "bd171ffba30c4e9272f57409c98faa4f", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "2cc4b959-4f56-5ed1-bcc1-29e32d21100b", "at": 1795691137.262027, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 78134, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.response", "role": "venue-repo", "actor": "venue-repo", "execution_id": "bd171ffba30c4e9272f57409c98faa4f", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "response"}, {"id": "fe0b5d8d-bf52-5d99-ad14-2f0c13e567da", "at": 1795691137.364814, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 78135, "layer": "worker", "c